{"findings":[{"criterionId":"cohesion","observation":"You signal your structure clearly with transitions such as 'One reason', 'Another reason why' and 'Finally', which helps the reader follow your argument. The link between your examples and your claim could be stronger. The Einstein, Musk and Jobs examples are listed one after another, and the sentence 'It's all in the head, that's where it all starts from' does not explicitly tie them back to the idea that imagination comes before knowledge. Try a sentence at the end of each example that states what it proves, and consider linking your final paragraph back to your opening analogy.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"One reason why imagination is greater than knowledge is that of the ability to imagine the unthinkable."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"It's all in the head, that's where it all starts from."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Finally, the last reason why imagination is more important than knowledge is that it can change you mentally and physically."}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"syntax","observation":"Most of your sentences are simple or compound, such as 'Imagination is alot more important than you think it is.' You do use some longer structures, for example 'Just constant knowledge is boring, but adding imagination is like adding sprinkles on to a boring vanilla cake.' Adding more complex sentences, such as ones with subordinate clauses introduced by 'because', 'although' or 'which', would show more control over sentence structure and make your reasoning more precise.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Just constant knowledge is boring, but adding imagination is like adding sprinkles on to a boring vanilla cake."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Imagination is alot more important than you think it is."}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"vocabulary","observation":"You use some effective, topic-appropriate words, such as 'unthinkable', 'innovative' and 'fuels the mind'. 'Imagination' is repeated very often, and general words such as 'cool', 'good' and 'things' appear in places where more precise language would work better. For example, 'cool things' could name the specific benefits you mean, such as creativity, problem-solving or emotional resilience. Replacing some repeated words with synonyms or more exact terms would widen your range.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Imagination is what fuels the mind to create knowledge."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"You can also do many cool things using your imagination"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"You should also try lucid dreaming to come up with brand new or more innovative idea"}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"phraseology","observation":"You use some useful collocations and expressions, such as 'come up with', 'in many ways' and 'pretty often'. Some phrases sound unnatural, though, such as 'imagined in it all in his head', which would read more naturally as 'imagined it all in his head'. Phrases like 'in many ways' and 'in different ways' are used repeatedly, so a wider set of connecting expressions would sound more fluent.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"He imagined in it all in his head."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Using your imagination is benefiting, motivating, changing all in different ways."}],"uncertainty":"medium"},{"criterionId":"grammar","observation":"Your writing shows good control of tense in most places. There are several errors to fix. 'How you think Elon Musk' needs the auxiliary 'do' to form a question. 'a mind filled of knowledge' should be 'filled with knowledge'. 'a better by work outing' should be 'a better person by working out'. In 'making you a better person or be a more fun person', the second verb should match the first, so 'or to be' is needed. 'work outing' should be 'working out' and 'going on jogs' is a valid form.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"How you think Elon Musk came up with the idea to start a colony on Mars?"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"If you had a mind filled of knowledge"},{"artifact":"work","quote":"motivating you to be a better by work outing and or going on jogs pretty often."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"by making you a better person or be a more fun person overall."}],"uncertainty":"low"},{"criterionId":"conventions","observation":"Your punctuation is mostly accurate, and you use a comma after the introductory phrase 'Quantum physicist'. There are several spelling and punctuation errors to correct. 'alot' should be 'a lot' in both places it appears. 'on to' should be 'onto'. The sentence 'Quantum physicist, Albert Einstein once said' would benefit from a comma after 'said' before the quotation, as in 'Einstein once said: \"Imagination...\"'. Add commas after introductory phrases such as 'For example' and 'As a result'.","excerpts":[{"artifact":"work","quote":"Imagination is alot more important than you think it is."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"adding sprinkles on to a boring vanilla cake."},{"artifact":"work","quote":"Quantum physicist, Albert Einstein once said, \"Imagination is more important than knowledge.\""}],"uncertainty":"low"}]}
